      "observed": "Learners observed a shot-put throw (live demonstration or video set on a school field) and plotted the ball's height against horizontal distance. They noted that the data points did not form a straight line — the curve rose, peaked, and fell symmetrically. When second differences of the height values were computed from a data table, they were constant (non-zero), signalling a quadratic rather than a linear relationship. Teacher should display both a linear and a quadratic data set side-by-side so learners can contrast first-difference (linear) vs. second-difference (quadratic) patterns before naming the rule.",
