      "learned": "Learners learned two special factorisation patterns: the Difference of Two Squares, a² − b² = (a − b)(a + b), and the Perfect Square Trinomial, a² + 2ab + b² = (a + b)². They practised recognising these patterns in expressions such as 4x² − 25 and x² + 10x + 25. They also learned the completing-the-square technique: rewriting x² + bx + c as (x + b/2)² − (b/2)² + c, which expresses the quadratic in vertex form and reveals the minimum or maximum value. Pedagogical note: Stress that completing the square is not merely a trick — it is the algebraic equivalent of the geometric square-completion diagram, and it will later be used to derive the quadratic formula.",
      "explained": "Learners explained how recognising the Difference of Two Squares pattern dramatically shortens factorisation: instead of searching for a factor pair, they simply identify a and b from a² − b² and write the two conjugate factors immediately. They also explained that completing the square on the shot-put height expression h = −x² + 6x + 1 (rewritten as −(x − 3)² + 10) reveals the maximum height of 10 metres at x = 3 metres — a real, physically meaningful result. Teacher should connect this back to the Driving Question Board: 'Which of our earlier questions does knowing the maximum height answer?' Allow learners to update their DQB entries."
