      "observed": "Learners observed a set of six quadratic expressions derived from earlier shamba and shot-put contexts and sorted them into three categories: (a) factorisable over the integers, (b) perfect square trinomials, and (c) difference of two squares. The sorting activity was done in pairs using printed cards. Learners then examined the graphical behaviour of each type — where the parabola crosses the x-axis (two points, one point tangent, or no real crossings) — using a pre-drawn set of parabola sketches. Teacher should allow 2 minutes of silent individual sorting before pair discussion, then cold-call three pairs to justify their categorisation.",
      "learned": "Learners learned that every quadratic expression of the form x² + bx + c can be written as a product of two brackets (x + p)(x + q) where p × q = c and p + q = b, consolidating the product-sum rule. They deepened understanding by connecting the signs of p and q to the signs of b and c: if c > 0 and b > 0, both factors are positive; if c > 0 and b < 0, both are negative; if c < 0, one factor is positive and one negative. Learners also revisited the a ≠ 1 case using the ac method and identified perfect-square and difference-of-squares expressions as efficient special cases. Pedagogical note: Use a colour-coding system (e.g., green for positive, red for negative) on the board to help learners track sign rules without memorising them as isolated facts.",
